1
00:00:00,262 --> 00:00:04,465
AlphaNIST: Emergent Assembly
Skills from Binary Task Rewards.

2
00:00:05,745 --> 00:00:10,070
We focus on the NIST board,
an assembly benchmark with twenty tasks,

3
00:00:10,070 --> 00:00:13,946
from gear meshing and nut threading
to peg and connector insertion.

4
00:00:14,711 --> 00:00:18,723
Four components make up the training
setup: binary task rewards,

5
00:00:18,723 --> 00:00:23,493
cached starting states, beta sampling,
and large-scale parallel experience.

6
00:00:24,216 --> 00:00:29,341
The task reward is binary: one for
a completed assembly, zero otherwise.

7
00:00:29,341 --> 00:00:34,178
There is no progress reward for reaching,
grasping, or partial insertion.

8
00:00:34,178 --> 00:00:37,343
The robot has to learn which
actions lead to completion.

9
00:00:38,085 --> 00:00:43,023
From loose parts alone, an untrained
policy may rarely reach insertion.

10
00:00:43,023 --> 00:00:47,873
A cached bank lets it also start with
parts already grasped or partly assembled,

11
00:00:47,873 --> 00:00:49,848
so it can practice finishing.

12
00:00:49,848 --> 00:00:52,660
These states are generated in simulation.

13
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:56,686
They specify where an attempt begins,
not which actions should follow.

14
00:00:57,428 --> 00:01:00,578
Not every starting state
needs equal practice.

15
00:01:00,578 --> 00:01:04,178
Beta sampling gives the most weight
to states the policy solves about

16
00:01:04,178 --> 00:01:07,728
half the time, while keeping
every state available.

17
00:01:07,728 --> 00:01:10,628
Learning from one state
can make another solvable;

18
00:01:10,628 --> 00:01:13,411
new outcomes then update
its sampling weight.

19
00:01:14,157 --> 00:01:19,107
Training runs in up to two hundred and
fifty-six thousand parallel environments,

20
00:01:19,107 --> 00:01:22,194
with four thousand environments per GPU.

21
00:01:22,194 --> 00:01:24,665
They collect experience
for a shared policy.

22
00:01:25,387 --> 00:01:28,350
Together, these components
train one policy across

23
00:01:28,350 --> 00:01:31,087
all twenty individual assembly tasks.

24
00:01:31,087 --> 00:01:34,362
It takes point clouds
and robot state as input,

25
00:01:34,362 --> 00:01:37,963
without demonstrations or
a separate controller for each part.

26
00:01:38,696 --> 00:01:41,383
Training across heterogeneous
assemblies gives

27
00:01:41,383 --> 00:01:45,446
higher training success than
learning each task separately.

28
00:01:45,446 --> 00:01:50,171
With binary task rewards, the shared
policy reaches ninety-eight percent,

29
00:01:50,171 --> 00:01:53,145
compared with eighty-one percent
for the separate policies.

30
00:01:53,922 --> 00:01:59,547
First, look at the full method in yellow:
binary task rewards with beta sampling.

31
00:01:59,547 --> 00:02:03,884
Evaluated success rises at every
tested scale, reaching about

32
00:02:03,884 --> 00:02:08,697
ninety-seven percent with two hundred and
fifty-six thousand parallel environments.

33
00:02:09,705 --> 00:02:14,205
Now add rewards for reaching
and alignment, keeping beta sampling.

34
00:02:14,205 --> 00:02:18,718
Success at the largest scale drops
by about twenty percentage points.

35
00:02:18,718 --> 00:02:21,455
The gap shows that more
detailed rewards do not

36
00:02:21,455 --> 00:02:24,286
necessarily make this
task easier to learn.

37
00:02:25,258 --> 00:02:29,308
Return to the full method,
and change only the sampling rule.

38
00:02:29,308 --> 00:02:32,233
Choosing cached starting
states uniformly lowers

39
00:02:32,233 --> 00:02:35,107
success by about thirteen
percentage points.

40
00:02:36,064 --> 00:02:39,927
Without a prescribed grasp,
the gripper holds this gear at an angle,

41
00:02:39,927 --> 00:02:43,054
turns it into alignment,
and lowers it onto the post.

42
00:02:46,083 --> 00:02:49,021
This grasp leaves too little
of the peg exposed.

43
00:02:49,021 --> 00:02:51,924
The robot sets it down
and grasps nearer the end.

44
00:02:52,624 --> 00:02:55,939
It moves this peg away from
the board edge before picking it up.

45
00:02:57,635 --> 00:03:01,812
The gripper presses the connector against
the board to turn it before insertion.

46
00:03:03,204 --> 00:03:05,904
Threading varies with nut size and reach.

47
00:03:05,904 --> 00:03:10,267
On the left, a fingertip taps
the small, nearby M4 nut.

48
00:03:10,267 --> 00:03:14,229
In the middle, the outside
of a finger turns the farther M12 nut.

49
00:03:14,229 --> 00:03:17,380
On the right, the gripper
circles the nearby M8 nut.

50
00:03:18,095 --> 00:03:22,145
The same training formulation also
works with several components.

51
00:03:22,145 --> 00:03:27,132
Now the binary task reward arrives only
when all active parts are assembled.

52
00:03:27,132 --> 00:03:31,007
There are many possible orders,
and the policy has to choose.

53
00:03:31,007 --> 00:03:36,270
In this ten-component run, it moves
parts aside, separates overlapping gears,

54
00:03:36,270 --> 00:03:40,174
and returns to the remaining
assemblies as space becomes available.

55
00:03:46,369 --> 00:03:48,556
Once a neighboring gear is in place,

56
00:03:48,556 --> 00:03:51,544
lowering this gear straight
down is not enough.

57
00:03:51,544 --> 00:03:55,615
The gripper turns it to line up the teeth,
then lets it settle into the mesh.

58
00:03:56,383 --> 00:04:00,045
Nearby parts also change
how the robot threads a nut.

59
00:04:00,045 --> 00:04:03,908
Beside an inserted Ethernet connector,
it uses short turns

60
00:04:03,908 --> 00:04:08,031
and repositions its fingers,
keeping its hand clear of the connector.

61
00:04:08,728 --> 00:04:12,728
We didn't prescribe how to turn the nut
or when to move another part aside.

62
00:04:12,728 --> 00:04:16,815
The policy learned these choices while
working toward a completed assembly.

63
00:04:16,815 --> 00:04:20,890
Shared training improves training success
over separate specialists.

64
00:04:20,890 --> 00:04:24,853
Binary task rewards outperform
the dense shaping we tested.

65
00:04:24,853 --> 00:04:29,615
And with multiple parts, the policy learns
which assemblies to work on and when.

66
00:04:29,615 --> 00:04:31,828
The reward specifies the outcome,

67
00:04:31,828 --> 00:04:34,512
while the robot learns
the maneuvers and their order.
